{"name":"Tiki Straw","hex":"#b9a37e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tiki","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A carved talisman in humanoid form, common to the cultures of the Pacific Ocean.","The characteristic style of tiki bars."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tiki"},{"term":"straw","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dried stalk of a cereal plant.","Such dried stalks considered collectively; this bulk matter may be a chief salable product, a by-product, fodder, bedding, or green manure, depending on region and on current market conditions."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of straw.","Of a pale, yellowish beige colour, like that of a dried straw."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/straw"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tiki","blocks":[{"text":"In Māori mythology, Tiki is the first man created by either Tūmatauenga or Tāne. He found the first woman, Marikoriko, in a pond; she seduced him, and he became the father of Hine-kau-ataata. By extension, a tiki is a large or small wooden, pounamu or other stone carving in humanoid form, although this is a somewhat archaic usage in the Māori language, where a tiki is usually a hei-tiki, a pendant worn around the neck. Hei-tiki are often considered taonga, especially if they are older and have been passed down throughout multiple generations. Carvings similar to tiki and coming to represent deified ancestors are found in most Polynesian cultures. They often serve to mark the boundaries of sacred or significant sites. The word has cognates in other Polynesian languages, such as tiʻi in Tahitian and kiʻi in Hawaiian. In the Western world, Tiki culture, a movement inspired by various Pacific cultures, has become popular in the 20th and 21st centuries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Tiki1905.jpg/330px-Tiki1905.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiki","wikidata":"Q13389032"},{"title":"Straw","blocks":[{"text":"Straw is an agricultural byproduct consisting of the dry stalks of cereal plants after the grain and chaff have been removed. It makes up about half of the yield by weight of cereal crops such as barley, oats, rice, rye and wheat. It has a number of different uses, including fuel, livestock bedding and fodder, thatching and basket making."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Rice_straw.jpg/330px-Rice_straw.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Straw","wikidata":"Q160066"},{"title":"Straw (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Straw is a colour, a tone of pale yellow, the colour of straw. The Latin word stramineus, with the same meaning, is often used in describing nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Strohr%C3%A4der.jpg/330px-Strohr%C3%A4der.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Straw_(colour)","wikidata":"Q7622361"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Kii_at_Puuhonua_O_Honaunau_01.jpg/500px-Kii_at_Puuhonua_O_Honaunau_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Kii_at_Puuhonua_O_Honaunau_01.jpg/500px-Kii_at_Puuhonua_O_Honaunau_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hawaiian kiʻi at Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKii_at_Puuhonua_O_Honaunau_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Tiki_Marquesas_Louvre_MH_87-50-1.jpg/500px-Tiki_Marquesas_Louvre_MH_87-50-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Tiki_Marquesas_Louvr